    "q-6054": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14270828/assets/15f17827141a0a31a7b748ab93c6811afcebd8c66781642554814f126aab8add.png)\n\nWhat is the inequality that describes the shaded region shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the equation of the corresponding line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$\n\n- The line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-1)$, so the $y$-intercept is $b=-1.$\n\n- The line passes through the points $(0,-1)$ and $(2,0)$, so its slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{0-(-1)}{2-0}=\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=\\frac{x}{2}-1.$\n\nFinally, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is solid, we will use an \"or equal to\" inequality. Since the shaded region is *above* the line, the inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line.\n\nSo, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge \\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
