    "q-113941": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "What is ${\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?",
      "worked_solution": "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ the lower bound is $a=4$ and the upper bound is $b=9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{9-4}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+k\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+\\frac{5k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
