    "q-42787": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 31",
      "problem": "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+4\\pi n,\\,x=a+4\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n,\\,\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n, & \\,\\frac{x}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n \\\\ \\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}+2\\pi n, & \\,\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}+2\\pi n \\\\ x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+4\\pi n, & \\,x=\\frac{13\\pi }{6}+4\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=\\frac{13\\pi }{6}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
