    "q-49515": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 32",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let $u=3x+4.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{5}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary positive constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
