    "q-16178": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 34",
      "problem": "Find the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $\\sqrt{3}x+y=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}x+y & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
