    "q-50509": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 36",
      "problem": "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{n-1}}{{12}^{n}}$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}$\n\nIII. ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n}$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{n-1}}{{12}^{n}}$ starts with $n=3.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{{6}^{4-1}}{{12}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{6}^{3-1}}{{12}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{6}^{3}}{{12}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{6}^{2}}{{12}^{3}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{6}^{3}}{{12}^{4}})(\\frac{{12}^{3}}{{6}^{2}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{6}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}$ starts with $n=2.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}}{5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}{{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series I and II converge.",
