    "q-76113": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 37",
      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=2x+\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$",
      "worked_solution": "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ 2x+\\sqrt{x} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =1-2x \\\\ x & =1-4x+4{x}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-5x+1 & =0 \\\\ (4x-1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChecking the solutions, we notice that only $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ satisfies the equation $2x+\\sqrt{x}=1.$ Therefore, we discard $x=1$ and conclude that ${f}^{-1}(1)=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{4}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2x+\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{4}) & =2+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{4})}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{4}) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
