    "q-73243": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 41",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t{e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((1-t){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-2){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=2.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(1-(2)){e}^{-(2)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
